===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[ale]] [[post]]}}

# A post displaying a pub sign; an [[alestake]].
#*'''1808''', [[w:Joseph Hall (bishop)|Joseph Hall]], ''Works''.
#*:O honor, farre beyond a brazen shrine,
#*:To sit with Tarleton on an '''ale post''''s signe
#*'''1957''', Alfred Edgar Coppard, ''It's Me, O Lord!''.
#*:I believe it was the George the Something or other, although I feel sure that no monarch of any era would have felt at home in the neighbourhood or been gratified by the '''alepost''''s honourable mention of him.
#*'''1991''', Simone Zelitch, ''The Confession of Jack Straw'', ISBN 0930773179.
#*:I looked for the '''alepost''' of the Fighting Cock, and found none.

====Usage notes====
Many nineteenth century dictionaries and wordlists define ''ale post'' as a maypole. This is an error which originated in Thomas Speght's [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/17060447 ''Life of Chaucer''] and was copied by others.<ref>[[w:Walter William Skeat|Walter William Skeat]], ''Chaucer's Works'', notes on the prologue to the ''Cantebury Tales''.</ref>

